// ============================================================================
// Hunt: OpenSSH "sshole" auth bypass on musl-based systems - oversized
//   passwords log into asterisk-locked accounts (fixed in OpenSSH 10.6p2)
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1078 (Valid Accounts - a locked account logging in with a
//   password is the flaw firing, not a user forgetting which account they
//   used)
// Data: SSH daemon logs (Syslog table as an example - point at wherever
//   your sshd auth logs land, e.g. /var/log/auth.log forwarded to Sentinel)
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-6-2026.html
// Logic: OpenSSH servers on musl-based Linux (Alpine, OpenWrt, Buildroot,
//   Yocto) let an attacker log into an account configured to block password
//   logins by submitting an oversized password of 257 to 1,024 characters.
//   Accounts are locked by replacing their password hash with an asterisk
//   in /etc/shadow, but a 2016 OpenSSH patch for the username-enumeration
//   flaw CVE-2016-6210 made the server hash the supplied password even for
//   accounts with no usable hash, so response times would not leak valid
//   usernames. On musl, crypt() returns an asterisk when hashing fails,
//   which happens for passwords longer than 256 bytes - so the oversized
//   password hashes to "*", OpenSSH compares it with the account's stored
//   "*", and the login succeeds. The flaw has existed since 2016 and was
//   found by independent researcher Özgün Kültekin; it was fixed in
//   OpenSSH 10.6p2, released November 5, 2026. No CVE ID had been published
//   for it as of November 6, and no exploitation in the wild had been
//   reported. Exploitation needs a musl-based system without PAM, password
//   authentication enabled, one asterisk-locked account, and another
//   account with a real password hash. It does not bypass accounts that
//   have valid password hashes, and glibc systems (Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora)
//   are not affected. Alpine is the sharp end: its openssh-server package
//   does not use PAM by default, its root account is asterisk-locked, and
//   its AWS and Azure images ship an alpine user with passwordless doas -
//   so a successful login to a locked account on those images can mean
//   root. Variant 1 hunts the core tell: an "Accepted password" event for
//   an account that can never legitimately log in with a password.
//   Variant 2 hunts accepted password logins on your musl hosts from
//   non-admin sources. Variant 3 hunts the probing pattern: failed
//   password attempts followed by a success for the same user and source.
//   Fill in your locked accounts, musl hostnames and admin sources before
//   running; the queries use no invented IoCs - no IPs, hashes or CVE ID
//   were published. Remediation: apk upgrade openssh on Alpine (or update
//   to OpenSSH 10.6p2), or disable SSH password authentication.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: accepted password logins for asterisk-locked accounts --
// An account whose /etc/shadow hash is "*" can never legitimately log in
// with a password. On a musl system without PAM, an oversized password
// (257-1,024 characters) makes musl crypt() return "*", which matches the
// stored "*", and OpenSSH lets the attacker in. Any "Accepted password"
// event for a locked account is therefore the flaw firing - not a user
// forgetting which account they used.
let LockedAccounts = dynamic(["root", "<locked-service-account-1>"]);
Syslog
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where ProcessName == "sshd"
| where SyslogMessage startswith "Accepted password"
| parse SyslogMessage with "Accepted password for " User " from " SourceIP " port " SourcePort " ssh2"
| where User in (LockedAccounts)
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, User, SourceIP, SourcePort
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: accepted password logins on musl-based hosts from non-admin sources --
// Alpine, OpenWrt, Buildroot and Yocto use musl; glibc systems (Debian,
// Ubuntu, Fedora) are not affected. List your musl hosts and admin sources
// below. On Alpine, a locked-account login can mean root: its
// openssh-server ships without PAM, root is asterisk-locked, and the AWS
// and Azure images give the alpine user passwordless doas - so check any
// hit for a doas/sudo command to root in the same session window.
let MuslHosts = dynamic(["<alpine-host-1>", "<openwrt-host-1>"]);
let AdminSources = dynamic(["<admin-jump-host-ip-1>", "<admin-workstation-subnet>"]);
Syslog
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where ProcessName == "sshd"
| where Computer in (MuslHosts)
| where SyslogMessage startswith "Accepted password"
| parse SyslogMessage with "Accepted password for " User " from " SourceIP " port " SourcePort " ssh2"
| where SourceIP !in (AdminSources)
| summarize LoginCount = count(),
            FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated), LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
    by Computer, User, SourceIP
| order by LoginCount desc

// -- Variant 3: failed password attempts followed by an accepted login (length probing) --
// An attacker finding the exact length window may leave failed attempts
// before the oversized password succeeds. A success for the same user and
// source right after failures - especially for a locked account - is the
// tell. No exploitation in the wild had been reported as of November 5,
// so treat hits as either the flaw or a password spray that got lucky.
let MuslHosts = dynamic(["<alpine-host-1>", "<openwrt-host-1>"]);
Syslog
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where ProcessName == "sshd"
| where Computer in (MuslHosts)
| where SyslogMessage startswith "Failed password" or SyslogMessage startswith "Accepted password"
| where SyslogMessage !contains "invalid user"
| parse SyslogMessage with * "password for " User " from " SourceIP " port " SourcePort " ssh2"
| summarize FailedCount = countif(SyslogMessage startswith "Failed password"),
            AcceptedCount = countif(SyslogMessage startswith "Accepted password"),
            FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated), LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
    by Computer, User, SourceIP
| where AcceptedCount > 0 and FailedCount > 0
| order by FailedCount desc
